# Chapter 1 – Introduction

# Microsoft Sentinel SOC Engineering Handbook

> Building a Cloud-Native Security Operations Center (SOC) using Microsoft Sentinel

---

# Purpose

Modern organizations generate enormous volumes of security telemetry every second. Authentication attempts, firewall events, endpoint detections, cloud activities, identity changes, and application logs all contribute to a continuously growing stream of operational data. While this information is valuable, it becomes useful only when it can be transformed into actionable security intelligence.

This handbook documents the design, implementation, and operation of a cloud-native Security Operations Center (SOC) using Microsoft Sentinel. It is intended to bridge the gap between theoretical knowledge and practical engineering by explaining not only *how* Microsoft Sentinel is configured, but also *why* architectural and detection decisions are made.

Rather than serving as a simple walkthrough, this repository is organized as a technical reference that follows the lifecycle of a security event—from data ingestion through investigation and response.

---

# Why This Project?

Many Microsoft Sentinel tutorials focus on completing individual tasks such as connecting a data source or creating an analytics rule. While those exercises are useful for learning the interface, they rarely explain how the individual components work together as part of an operational SOC.

This project was created to provide a broader engineering perspective.

It explains:

- How security logs are collected
- How Azure Monitor processes telemetry
- How Azure Log Analytics stores and indexes data
- How Microsoft Sentinel performs detection and correlation
- How Kusto Query Language (KQL) is used for threat hunting
- How incidents are investigated
- How detections can be improved for production environments

The goal is to understand the reasoning behind each configuration and detection rather than simply reproducing a lab.

